# shellcheck shell=bash
#
# Rebuild PATH from a known base, early, so that OUR entries win.
#
# ── THE PROBLEM ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# /etc/profile is read whenever a login shell starts -- and login shells start
# far more often than you would think: every tmux pane, every VSCode integrated
# terminal, every ssh session.
#
# On macOS, /etc/profile runs:
#
#     eval $(/usr/libexec/path_helper -s)
#
# path_helper does not append. It REBUILDS PATH from /etc/paths and
# /etc/paths.d, then tacks whatever you already had onto the end. So a tool
# directory you deliberately put at the front comes back at the rear -- an
# entry at position 1 returns at position 13. You get the system vim instead of
# yours, the system git, the system tmux.
#
# We cannot edit /etc/profile on most of these machines. So instead: reset PATH
# to a minimal base and source /etc/profile ourselves, early, before any of our
# own entries exist. Everything we add afterwards then lands in front of the
# system, deterministically.
#
# Ref: https://superuser.com/a/970847 -- tmux *should* start a login shell,
#      even though that is exactly what triggers this.
#
# ── THIS FILE DOES THREE JOBS ──────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Only the first is macOS-specific. Do not "optimise" it away on Linux, and do
# not gate it on interactivity (see the next section).
#
#   1. macOS: undo path_helper's demotion of our PATH entries.
#
#   2. EVERYWHERE: dedupe. Starting from a known base is what stops PATH
#      accumulating repeated entries. Skip it and the inherited PATH survives,
#      path_helper appends it behind the system dirs, and bashrc prepends our
#      entries again -- leaving two copies of everything.
#
#   3. LINUX: load /etc/profile.d/* for shells that would otherwise never see
#      it. On talapas, /etc/profile.d/slurm.sh is the ONLY thing putting
#      ${SLURM_ROOT}/bin on PATH and setting LD_LIBRARY_PATH for a remote
#      command -- sshd does not supply it -- so `ssh talapas squeue` depends on
#      this file running. On Ubuntu, /etc/environment supplies /usr/local/bin,
#      /snap/bin and /usr/games the same way.
#
# ── DO NOT GATE THIS ON `[[ $- == *i* ]]` ──────────────────────────────────
#
# Yo, you might think this is for interactive only, but you'd be wrong. It's
# needed for non-interactive-ssh+ext as well. For example: Gating this breaks
# `ssh talapas squeue` outright and doubles PATH entries everywhere.
#
# ── ORDERING INVARIANT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Everything that adds to PATH must run AFTER this file. Today that means the
# */path fragments (via all/main-common) and the pixi line, both further down
# bash/bashrc. Move this file later, or those earlier, and PATH silently ends
# up in system-first order with our tools shadowed.
#
# ── CONDA WAS THE SYMPTOM, NOT THE CAUSE ───────────────────────────────────
#
# path_helper demotes ANY user-added PATH entry. Conda was simply what stood
# there first, back when this file was written. Pixi stands there now. This
# file stays load-bearing even after the conda block at the bottom goes away.
#
# ── MORE INFO ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# bash/README.Interactive-Soup.md -- glossary, the three-zone model, the host
# matrix, and the dated history of how all of the above was worked out.

# /etc/environment first: on some hosts (Ubuntu) that is where the base PATH
# comes from.
PATH="/bin:/usr/bin"
if [ -f /etc/environment ]; then
    # shellcheck source=/dev/null
    source /etc/environment > /dev/null 2> /dev/null
fi

# Redirected: this runs in the scp/sftp path too, and /etc/profile.d/*.sh can
# print. Stray output on stdout corrupts the transfer protocol.
if [ -f /etc/profile ]; then
    # shellcheck source=/dev/null
    source /etc/profile > /dev/null 2> /dev/null
fi

# Re-activate conda so it sits in front again, inside tmux.
#
# conda: DEPRECATED in favour of pixi. Kept deliberately -- teaching demos, and
# as a fallback until pixi has a few uneventful years behind it. Targeted for
# possible removal in 2028. No-op where conda is absent.
if command -v conda >/dev/null 2>&1 && [[ -n "$TMUX" ]]; then
    conda deactivate
    conda activate base
fi
